THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_003 computes the first pass in the framework 'that contains all warrants but not their defeats.' But a warrant w targets an attack edge e — w is an argument that *defeats* e. If defeats are not applied, then w is just an unattached argument: nothing attacks e, so w's acceptance is determined solely by attacks on w itself. However, a warrant w that defeats attack A→B is itself an argument that can be attacked. In the first pass (no defeats applied), w has no special role, so w is accepted iff no undefeated attacker of w exists. But the attackers of w are the same arguments; without applying defeats, *all* attacks in the base framework are active, including attacks on the original arguments that w was meant to defend. Thus the first pass is exactly the original grounded extension computed on the expanded framework — and warrants accepted there are those with no attacker. A warrant attacked by an argument that w itself would defeat is rejected in pass 1 (because w's defeat is not applied), even though in pass 2 w would survive. This two-pass stratification does not correspond to a single fixpoint: the second pass applies defeats from warrants accepted in pass 1, but those defeats can change the acceptance of arguments that attack other warrants, which were already settled in pass 1 and are not recomputed. Reinstatement is violated: a warrant w2 attacked by argument A, where A is defeated only by a warrant w1 accepted in pass 1, is rejected in pass 1 but would be accepted if pass 1 used pass-1 defeats — but it does not. The single fixpoint guarantee is lost because the semantics is defined by two sequential non-interacting fixpoints rather than one.

TARGET:
Make attack-defeater warrants themselves subject to the grounded fixpoint before they are allowed to defeat an attack. A registered warrant w that targets an attack edge e becomes effective only if w is accepted in the grounded extension of the framework that contains all warrants but not their defeats; then compute a second pass where those accepted warrants' defeats are applied. This avoids self-defeating warrants and keeps determinism because the two passes are each deterministic fixpoints. Criticism: circular dependencies between warrant acceptance and defeat application may require a careful stratification; a warrant that is only accepted because of its own defeat is rejected.